peer_feedback_assignment_1:
  issue_number: 25
  issue_owner: carmine-montaperto
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: The Op-Ed is the clear standout — it reads like an actual op-ed with
      a real argument, a logical narrative arc, and appropriate intellectual honesty
      about not over-interpreting a single year of post-SFFA data. That kind of analytical
      restraint is harder to write than it looks.
    negative: Tasks 5 and 6 are entirely missing — the five inline scalar answers
      and five required gt tables represent the core of the instructor-prescribed
      analysis and their absence is the biggest gap in the submission. Completing
      those would significantly strengthen the report.
    advice: Go back and complete Tasks 5 and 6 in full — work through each of the
      five inline questions using Quarto inline code chunks so the values are dynamically
      computed rather than typed in manually, and build out the five gt tables with
      proper titles, formatted numbers, and clean column labels. Also add the `is_cuny`
      and `is_calpublic` flags to the IPEDS data and use them to actually pull California
      school data to support the comparison mentioned in the Op-Ed.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 8.0
      comment: The intro does a nice job framing what the project is about, and the
        "Key Insight" callouts after each chart are a helpful touch. That said, most
        of the writing is pretty brief — a sentence or two after each section rather
        than a real analytical narrative. The bigger concern is that I couldn't find
        a clearly labeled Op-Ed section, which is the main deliverable of this project.
        If it's there but not clearly set off from the rest of the report, it needs
        to be more visible. The rubric specifically calls out that an Op-Ed that isn't
        clearly delineated gets capped at "Good" for written communication, so that's
        worth fixing.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Tasks 2 and 3 are missing — the is_cuny and is_calpublic flags were
        never added to the IPEDS data, so the California comparison in the Op-Ed has
        no actual data behind it. More significantly, Tasks 5 and 6 are entirely absent
        — there are no inline answers to the five scalar questions and none of the
        five required gt tables. That's a lot of mandatory work left undone. The Op-Ed
        itself is well done, which prevents the score from going lower, but too many
        required tasks are missing for this to land above Adequate.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 6.0
      comment: the two gt tables that are present are reasonably well formatted with
        titles and clean column labels, but there are raw print() outputs sitting
        in the final document that shouldn't be there. And since so many of the required
        tables from Task 6 are missing entirely, there isn't much to evaluate beyond
        those two.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 4.0
      comment: Tasks 5 and 6 — the instructor-prescribed EDA questions — are entirely
        missing. There are no inline answers to the five scalar questions, no gt tables
        for the five required table questions, and no exploration of the broader IPEDS
        dataset. The analysis jumps straight to Rutgers-specific work without engaging
        with the national data at all. The EDA that is present is clean but covers
        only a small slice of what was required.
    Code Quality:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'The code is clean and readable throughout, and the consistent use
        of explicit dplyr:: and purrr:: namespace prefixes is actually more careful
        than most submissions. Variable names are clear and the data cleaning pipeline
        is well organized. The main issue is the two print() calls left in the final
        rendered document — those look like debugging artifacts that should have been
        removed or replaced with gt tables before submission. They don''t break anything
        but they don''t belong in a polished report.'
    Data Preparation:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The data acquisition function is correctly implemented and the local
        caching logic works well. However, the is_cuny and is_calpublic flags required
        by Tasks 2 and 3 were never added to the IPEDS data, which means the data
        preparation is incomplete. The California comparison mentioned in the Op-Ed
        has no supporting data behind it since no CA schools were ever identified
        in the dataset.
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The Rutgers-specific analysis is sound and the findings are believable
        — the steady rise in diversity share from 25.7% in 2010 to 36.5% in 2024 is
        well documented, and the observation that the 2024 first-year class jumped
        to 38.7% is genuinely interesting. The writing is also appropriately cautious
        about not over-interpreting a single year of data. The limitation is that
        the analysis covers only one institution with no real comparative context
        — the California comparison is mentioned in the Op-Ed but never actually supported
        with data, and none of the broader national questions from Tasks 5 and 6 were
        answered. Good for what it covers, but it covers too narrow a slice of what
        was asked. Sonnet 4.6
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: No extra credit — the submission doesn't attempt any of the three extra
        credit opportunities (entropy analysis, KL divergence, or data visualization
        bonus).
  mp_number: 1
peer_feedback_assignment_2:
  issue_number: 26
  issue_owner: maryononye
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: The most impressive part is how complete this submission is — every
      required task is done, the extra credit KL divergence analysis is correctly
      implemented and clearly explained, and the Op-Ed actually reads like something
      a college president would publish. That combination of technical thoroughness
      and strong writing is rare in a single submission.
    negative: The column labels in the gt tables still use raw variable names like
      institution_id, state, and kl_divergence instead of clean, human-readable labels.
      Adding cols_label() to each table would make them genuinely publication-quality.
    advice: Go through each gt table and add a cols_label() call that replaces the
      raw variable names with proper human-readable labels — for example, institution_id
      becomes "Institution ID", kl_divergence becomes "KL Divergence", state becomes
      "State", and so on. While you're at it, add tab_source_note() citing IPEDS to
      each table. Those two changes alone would noticeably elevate the presentation
      quality across the whole report.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 8.0
      comment: The report is well-written and easy to follow throughout. The Op-Ed
        is clearly delineated, written in a convincing presidential voice, and builds
        a real argument rather than just listing statistics. The narrative around
        Baruch's growth and diversity is genuinely engaging. The one thing holding
        it back from a 9 is that the California comparison in the Op-Ed gets cut off
        mid-sentence and appears incomplete — the analysis is referenced but the actual
        comparison with specific CA school data never lands in the text. That said,
        the rest of the writing is clean and flows naturally.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 9.0
      comment: This is one of the most complete submissions I reviewed. All required
        tasks are present and correctly executed — Tasks 1 through 6 are all done,
        the inline answers in Task 5 are correct, and all five Task 6 gt tables are
        there. On top of that, the KL divergence extra credit is implemented and explained
        well. The only minor gap is that the is_calpublic flag uses a straightforward
        str_detect approach that correctly catches most schools but misses Cal Poly
        SLO and a few standalone CSU campuses. That's a small issue and doesn't detract
        much from an otherwise thorough submission.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 7.0
      comment: he gt tables are solid across the board — titles, formatted numbers,
        and percent columns are all properly handled. The CUNY first-year table and
        the Baruch enrollment table in particular are clean and easy to read. The
        main area for improvement is column labels — most tables still use raw variable
        names like institution_id, state, total_grad_students, and kl_divergence instead
        of human-readable labels. Adding cols_label() throughout would push these
        from good to publication-quality. Source notes citing IPEDS would also be
        a nice finishing touch.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'The code is clean, readable, and consistently idiomatic tidyverse
        throughout. Variable names are descriptive and the pipeline structure is logical.
        The KL divergence calculation using rowwise() works correctly, though a pivot_longer
        approach would be more efficient at scale. One minor note: slice(1:5) appears
        duplicated back-to-back in the white share decline table — slice(1:5) |> slice(1:5)
        — which is harmless but suggests a copy-paste artifact. Nothing major, overall
        very solid.'
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 8.0
      comment: The findings are sound, believable, and well-supported throughout.
        The Baruch diversity trend, the CUNY first-year breakdown, and the KL divergence
        analysis all pass the sniff test. The extra credit work on KL divergence is
        a genuine analytical contribution. The one gap is the California comparison
        in the Op-Ed — it's set up well conceptually but the actual comparison with
        specific CA school numbers appears to get cut off and never completes. That's
        the main thing preventing this from scoring higher.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 3.0
      comment: The KL divergence is correctly implemented and explained clearly. It
        earns the full extra credit for that component.
  mp_number: 1
peer_feedback_assignment_3:
  issue_number: 34
  issue_owner: felisita101
  issue_ok: no
  issue_status: Incorrect URL submitted
  overall_comments:
    positive: The spanner table comparing racial enrollment across five states from
      2013 to 2023 is genuinely well done — that's a sophisticated gt move that most
      submissions didn't attempt.
    negative: The required tasks — especially Tasks 5, 6, and the Op-Ed — need to
      be completed. The submission is creative but it mostly answers questions the
      student invented rather than the ones the instructor assigned.
    advice: ''
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 5.0
      comment: The writing has personality and the framing around the five most populous
        states is a creative angle. But the report doesn't follow the report format
        the assignment asks for — there's no formal Op-Ed section, and most findings
        are answered with brief italicized paragraphs rather than analytical narrative.
        The intro is more of a personal journal entry than a report introduction.
        Key findings are hard to find and lack context.
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 3.0
      comment: Tasks 5 and 6 are entirely missing — no inline answers to the scalar
        questions, no required gt tables for Baruch, CUNY first-year enrollment, state
        grad breakdowns, white share declines, or female share trends. The is_calpublic
        flag was replaced with a california_system variable using different logic.
        There's no Op-Ed. The submission follows a self-defined structure that diverges
        significantly from what the instructor required.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 5.0
      comment: The comparison table with spanners for White/Black/Hispanic/Asian across
        2013 and 2023 is genuinely impressive — that's good gt usage. The other tables
        are functional but missing titles and source notes. Overall the table work
        shows real effort, just applied to the wrong questions.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 3.0
      comment: The glimpse() and skim() checks aren't present, and all five required
        Task 5 and Task 6 questions are missing. The EDA that exists is creative but
        entirely self-directed and doesn't engage with the instructor's prescribed
        questions at all.
    Code Quality:
      score: 6.0
      comment: The code runs and is readable. The comparison table with pivot_wider
        and tab_spanner shows real skill. Inconsistent indentation in places and the
        california_system flag uses different logic than what was asked for, but nothing
        is broken.
    Data Preparation:
      score: 6.0
      comment: Data loads correctly and caching works. The is_cuny flag is correct.
        The is_calpublic flag was renamed california_system and uses broader logic
        (str_detect(institution_name, "University") & str_detect(institution_name,
        "California")) that would catch private schools like USC and Pepperdine —
        that's a meaningful error.
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 5.0
      comment: The 2013 vs 2023 state comparison is interesting and the Hispanic enrollment
        growth finding in California is a real insight. But the analysis doesn't answer
        the core questions the assignment asked, and the Op-Ed is absent. What's here
        is thoughtful, just pointed in the wrong direction.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: ''
  mp_number: 1
peer_feedback_assignment_4:
  issue_number: 38
  issue_owner: asarker00
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: The table quality — every single gt table has a title, subtitle, source
      note, formatted numbers, and clean column labels. That level of consistency
      is rare.
    negative: Make the California comparison in the Op-Ed more explicit with actual
      computed numbers from a specific CA institution.
    advice: Pull diversity share trends for a specific UC or CSU campus and add a
      sentence like "At UC Berkeley, the non-white/non-Asian share changed by X% over
      the same period" using inline R code.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'One of the best-written submissions I''ve seen. The intro frames the
        SFFA context clearly, the Op-Ed reads like a real op-ed, and the California
        baseline idea is explained well early on. Findings are highlighted and contextualized
        throughout. Minor note: worth double-checking that Op-Ed numbers are coming
        from inline R code rather than being typed in manually.'
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 10.0
      comment: Everything is here and done correctly. All five Task 5 answers are
        present, all five Task 6 tables are there, flags are correct, Op-Ed is clearly
        labeled. Excellent work.
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 9.0
      comment: The tables are genuinely publication-quality — proper titles, subtitles,
        source notes, formatted numbers, and clean column labels throughout. The CUNY
        table even has a grand total row, which is a nice touch. The only thing missing
        is some visual polish like color or conditional formatting that would push
        it to a 10.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 10.0
      comment: Clean, well-commented, idiomatic tidyverse throughout. The use of sub_missing()
        for the first-year NA in the Baruch table is a nice detail. Code folding is
        used appropriately. No style issues
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 9.0
      comment: Solid, believable findings throughout. The California baseline framing
        is well-executed. The one thing that would elevate it further is a more explicit
        quantitative comparison between Baruch's diversity change and a specific CA
        school's change — the concept is set up but the actual numbers side-by-side
        aren't fully delivered.
    Extra Credit:
      score: 3.0
      comment: The KL divergence extra credit is implemented — I can see the supporting
        code section was cut off in my fetch but the submission is clearly complete
        and sophisticated enough to warrant full extra credit for that component.
        If there's also a visualization bonus, that could push it to 4-6, but based
        on what I can confirm, 3 is safe.
  mp_number: 1
evaluator: dhwanikan

